@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title Testing strategy — four layers, speed traded for fidelity

' purpose: the four test layers, and the failure modes the def-level bash suites
'          have actually exhibited — every one of them a test that passed
' covers: internal/contract/**
' covers: e2e/**
' covers: internal/tmux/**
' covers: defs/tests/**
' covers: defs/*/test.sh
' covers: defs/*/tests/*.sh
' covers: defs/sidecars/mitmproxy/test.sh
'
' Image resolution for these suites (proveo_test_image, and why :latest could only
' ever mean "whatever the registry last published") is
' _spec/_devops/image-lineage-and-publish.puml.
'
' ── FOUR WAYS A def SUITE REPORTED PASS WHILE TESTING NOTHING ───────────────
' Recorded because each cost real time, and because all four are shapes rather
' than one-off bugs.
'
' 1. A SUITE THAT REBUILT WHAT IT TESTED. claudecode's phase 1 used to BUILD the
'    image it then asserted against, with a bare `docker build` and no BASE_IMAGE
'    build-arg — so `ARG BASE_IMAGE=proveo/base-node-lsp:latest`, the Dockerfile's
'    default, applied. That is the PUBLISHED base. Every run therefore threw away
'    the local lineage that build.sh and ensure.sh exist to establish, overwrote
'    the operator's image with a registry-based one, and then asserted against the
'    result.
'    It surfaced as `bun: command not found`: bun was added to proveo/base-node
'    AFTER the last base-node-lsp publish, so a locally built claudecode had it
'    and the one this phase substituted did not. Rebuilding by hand "fixed" it
'    exactly until the next run of the suite clobbered it again — twice, before
'    the mechanism was found.
'    opencode and cursor verify AVAILABILITY here and never build; claudecode now
'    matches them. Building is build.sh's job, and it is the only caller that
'    knows how to resolve the base chain: `proveo build claudecode`.
'
' 2. A MISSING HELPER THAT STILL PRODUCED A NUMBER. test_config.sh had called
'    `run_timeout` since it was written and claudecode's helpers never defined it
'    (opencode's and cursor's both do). It failed as `run_timeout: command not
'    found`, and because the caller is
'      BAD_RULES=$(run_timeout ... docker run ... | grep -c 'mcp__\*' || true)
'    the empty pipeline still produced "0" — so the wildcard-MCP-rule assertion
'    reported PASS without ever starting a container. A test that cannot fail.
'
' 3. TWO NEGATIVES OVER A PATH THAT NO LONGER EXISTED. In test_config.sh both
'    tool-permission checks are `assert_failure`. While they pointed at the
'    retired /opt/claudecode/defaults/agents, `grep` found no directory, failed,
'    and both reported PASS having verified nothing. A POSITIVE assertion now goes
'    first, so a missing tree fails loudly instead of turning the two negatives
'    vacuous again.
'
' 4. A `set -e` CASCADE THAT HID NINE PHASES. test_config.sh sed'd
'    `seed_claude_subagents` out of /entrypoint.sh, but the renderer had already
'    become `render_subagents` in the SHARED lib, called from proveo_seed's
'    per-harness case. The old spelling matched nothing, so `eval ""` succeeded
'    and the call died `command not found` (127) — which under `set -e` took the
'    whole suite down after that phase. Every phase from Workspace onward never
'    ran at all.
'
' ── AND ONE THAT REPORTED FAIL WHILE TESTING FINE ──────────────────────────
' The inverse shape, and it reads as a broken product rather than a broken test.
'
' A ROOT CONTAINER WRITING INTO A ""t.TempDir()"". e2e/scope_mounts_test.go probes
' the provisioning paths with ""docker run --user root"" and the temp dir bind-
' mounted at /w. Root is DELIBERATE — those paths install packages — but anything
' the install leaves behind is owned by root, and Go's TempDir cleanup runs as the
' TEST USER. MEASURED: the absent-node_modules subtest supplies no node_modules,
' so ""ensure_dependency_trees"" auto-installs, and ""pnpm install"" left
' /w/.pnpm-store and /w/node_modules as root:root —
'   TempDir RemoveAll cleanup: unlinkat …/.pnpm-store/v10/files/7b: permission denied
' on a subtest whose own assertion had already PASSED. The signal points at the
' code under test; the cause is the probe's own leftovers.
'
' Turning auto-install off is not the fix: ""_proveo_auto_install_enabled || return 0""
' is the FIRST line of ensure_dependency_trees, so disabling it silences the very
' warning the subtest asserts.
'
' So ONE helper — ""rootLibProbe"" — owns the invocation and hands ownership back
' with a ""trap … EXIT"", which fires on the ""exit 1""s the probe scripts use to
' report failure too. Without that, the first REAL failure here would be followed
' by a cleanup failure masking it. The file's other probes already avoided this by
' running as ""--user hostUIDGID(t)""; the four root ones now clean up instead.
'
' ── THE ROSTER IS READ FROM THE IMAGE, NEVER RESTATED ───────────────────────
' Subagents are no longer under /opt/<harness>/defaults/agents. They are one
' shared tree at /opt/proveo/subagents with a per-harness _roster.json, and
' cursor's and opencode's test_defaults.sh went on asserting the retired path —
' 10 failures against an image that was built correctly, which reads as a broken
' image rather than a stale test.
'
' So the roster is READ FROM THE IMAGE. A list copied into a test drifts silently
' the moment the roster changes; cecli's test.sh already took this approach
' ("read from the image, never restated here") and did not go stale.
' `tools:` and `readonly: true` moved with them, into the per-harness FRONTMATTER
' (_frontmatter/<harness>/<agent>.yaml) that render_subagents applies to the
' shared body — the bodies never carried either, so they are asserted there.
' See _spec/defs/agent-definition-sharing.puml.
'
' ── WHAT A def SUITE IS ALLOWED TO ASSERT ───────────────────────────────────
' These suites assert what the IMAGE carries. When cursor's test_tools.sh was
' INVERTED on 2026-09-05 to expect NO docker binaries, that was correct for
' exactly this reason: the image now deliberately carries none. Whether a sandbox
' can still REACH a daemon is a different question, owned by e2e/sbx_test.go's
' assertSandboxSuppliesDocker, which was deliberately not inverted.
'
' ── WHEN A def SUITE MAY SKIP RATHER THAN ASSERT ────────────────────────────
' Only when the property is IMPOSSIBLE on the host running the suite, never when
' it is merely inconvenient — and the reason must be printed, so a skip reads as
' a fact about the machine rather than a hole in the coverage.
'
' The case that set this: `[sol] solc is installed` failed on every arm64 run
' with `OSError: [Errno 8] Exec format error` on solc-0.8.20, beside solc-select's
' own `.arm64_warning_shown` marker. solc publishes NO linux-arm64 binary, so
' solc-select installs the x86-64 one and exec cannot succeed. No Dockerfile
' change can cause or fix that, and the failure made `mise run test-images` exit
' non-zero — which ABORTS the run before cursor's and opencode's suites, so one
' impossible assertion was hiding two whole suites.
'
' Scoped as narrowly as the fact allows: only the EXEC is skipped, only on
' aarch64/arm64. `solc-select versions` still asserts the version is installed,
' solc's ABSENCE from the base variants is still asserted, and on x86-64 the
' original assertion runs unchanged. The skip prints
' "solc publishes no linux-arm64 build; solc-select installs the x86-64 one".
'
' A note on the judgement, because this is a test edited to make a suite pass:
' the honest test of that move is whether the assertion could EVER hold here. It
' cannot. Had it been failing for a reason the tree controls, the fix would have
' been the tree.
'
' Two smaller conventions worth keeping:
'   * the baked uid/gid is asserted against the Dockerfile's `ARG USER_ID` (1000,
'     named "claude" in claudecode), overridable via EXPECTED_UID;
'   * NODE_ENV is asserted ABSENT, because baking it would make the agent's own
'     `npm`/`pnpm install` skip devDependencies — the project's .env is the only
'     place allowed to set it.
' The Claude-in-Chrome phase runs a FAKE host relay inside the container in place
' of `proveo run`'s, so the chain is driven end to end without a browser
' (_spec/defs/claudecode/chrome-bridge.puml).
'
' ── THE GO SUITES SKIP FOR ONE REASON: A MISSING PREREQUISITE ───────────────
' The `//go:build e2e` tag IS the opt-in. A suite that compiles under it must
' decide for ITSELF whether this host can run it, so the only reason to skip is a
' missing PREREQUISITE — PTY driver, container backend, harness image, credential
' — never an unset ceremonial flag. A green run with every test skipped is then an
' honest statement about the host, and one that `go test -v` prints the reason for.
'
' The floor, and why each check is shaped the way it is:
'   * requireTmux — tmux is the PTY driver every harness run needs.
'   * requireDocker — checks the CLI on PATH, deliberately NOT the daemon: every
'     suite here shells out to `docker`, and the endpoint (context or DOCKER_HOST)
'     is resolved per-run by dockerHost.
'   * harnessImage — building is a maintainer action (`mise run build <target>`),
'     so a missing image is a skip, not a failure.
'   * requireHarnessCredential — the credential VARIABLES come from the def's own
'     manifest (`env[].secret`, in declaration order), never restated, so a harness
'     that changes what it authenticates with cannot drift out of the guard. It
'     resolves through hostEnvValue rather than os.Getenv, because this suite
'     accepts a key from the process environment OR the repo .env, and checking
'     only the first skipped runs the developer had in fact provisioned.
'   * requireReviewTier — mirrors reviewSupported in cmd/proveo, which is the
'     source of truth: linux only, and a unix:// DOCKER_HOST. `proveo run` already
'     warns on an unsupported host that "the consent gate cannot be reached from
'     the inspector on this host, so every new connection will be DENIED without a
'     prompt". Without the skip the test does not fail fast, it TIMES OUT waiting
'     for an overlay that will never render — 90 seconds spent to report a host
'     capability as a defect.
'
' ── :local BEFORE :latest, WHEREVER A SUITE RESOLVES AN IMAGE ───────────────
' `mise run build` tags a local build :local and only a publish moves :latest, so
' harnessImageName prefers :local and falls back to :latest (overridable per target
' with PROVEO_TEST_IMAGE_<TARGET>). Defaulting to :latest skipped these suites on a
' machine that had just built the thing under test — and on one that had also
' pulled, ran the PUBLISHED image while the report read as though it covered the
' local build. anyImagePresent applies the same rule to guards and teardown: a
' guard naming one tag skipped the entire suite whenever the developer had built
' the other, and a cleanup naming one removed nothing.
'
' ── A FIFTH VACUOUS SHAPE: ZERO OF N, REPORTED AS ALL OF N ──────────────────
' The four above are bash. This one is Go, and the same shape. The two backends
' keep a secret out of the launch plan by different means: docker declares the
' `proveo-brokered` sentinel in argv, while sbx puts nothing there at all and hands
' values to `sbx secret set` on stdin. Read blind, an sbx plan satisfied the
' sentinel loop by containing no keys to check — zero of N, reported as "all
' forwarded". assertPlanIsolation now branches on isSbxArgv: on the docker branch
' `declared == 0` is itself an error, and on the sandbox branch ANY provider key in
' argv is an error, because argv is visible to anyone listing processes.
'
' ── AN ASSERTION MUST EXPLAIN ITS OWN FAILURE ───────────────────────────────
' e2e/instrument_test.go is the shared instrumentation for the tmux-driven layer,
' and every assertion that can time out fails through it, because the failures that
' cost the most to diagnose are the ones that report nothing useful. Three rules,
' each learned from a failure that hid its own cause:
'   1. Snapshot the scrollback WHILE the session is alive. Capturing only at the
'      moment of failure returns "no server running" once the run has exited, which
'      hides whatever it printed on the way out.
'   2. A single empty capture is not death. tmux returns nothing mid-redraw and
'      during long output (an image pull), so only CONSECUTIVE misses count —
'      deadAfter = 4.
'   3. Always include exited containers (`docker ps -a`). "Started and died" and
'      "never started" are different faults and look identical without it.
'
' A credential the AGENT cannot spend is a condition of the host, not a defect, so
' the watcher's Fatalf turns a pane matching credentialUnavailable into a SKIP with
' the diagnostics attached. That pattern is deliberately narrow — `credit balance
' is too low`, `insufficient credits|quota|balance`, `authentication_error`,
' `invalid x-api-key`, `invalid api key` — because broadening it to any early exit
' would let a real adapter defect leave as a skip.
'
' Two waits carry the same reasoning. waitForContainerShell treats the container
' prompt as the signal that the WHOLE topology came up — sidecars, networks, CA
' trust — because proveo only hands the PTY over once the container is running, so
' anything failing earlier fails HERE with the scrollback attached rather than as a
' confusing timeout further down. It matches ":/app$" (or "#"), not "$ ": tmux trims
' trailing whitespace, so a space-suffixed match never fires. /app is the one shared
' workdir; /workspace stays accepted only so an older image does not fail the wait.
' Both are DOCKER mount points — a sandbox run mounts a workspace at its own host
' path, so a caller that has not pinned PROVEO_SBX=off waits out its deadline for a
' prompt that cannot appear. acceptChoicePrompt anchors on the key hint
' ("enter accept", 90s) rather than the form's title: the title is copy, it has been
' reworded once, and that reword broke a test that waited on it.
'
' ── TEARDOWN IS SHARED STATE ────────────────────────────────────────────────
' `proveo clean --force` removes EVERY proveo container, so running it after each
' test can tear down a sibling still coming up. forceCleanIfCrowded therefore only
' sweeps once at least cleanThreshold = 10 containers carrying the
' `proveo.egress.session` label have accumulated; below that the risk outweighs the
' benefit and the suite-level clean handles it.
'
' ── THE ENVIRONMENT A SUITE HANDS ITS CHILD ─────────────────────────────────
' Three traps, all of which present as "the session started and printed nothing":
'   * env(1) on BSD/macOS requires every -u option BEFORE any NAME=value operand,
'     so the -u list has to come first or env tries to exec "-u" as the utility and
'     the session dies with no output.
'   * Overriding HOME hides ~/.docker and with it the active context, so the CLI
'     silently falls back to /var/run/docker.sock and every docker call — starting
'     with the image pull — hangs. Pin the endpoint explicitly: dockerHost reads
'     $DOCKER_HOST, else `docker context inspect`.
'   * An isolated HOME is also what makes driving the choice prompt deterministic.
'     A shared HOME would leak into the developer's real settings AND skip the
'     prompt on every later run.
' PROVEO_SBX=off belongs in the same list wherever a test inspects proveo's OWN
' egress topology — the "-egress" container and the broker.env bind-mounted into
' it. claudecode takes the sandbox backend wherever sbx is installed, and sbx brings
' its own credential proxy instead, so unpinned there is no egress container to find
' and the probe burns its 120s timeout before failing for an unrelated reason.
' Where the provider must be detected from a workspace .env alone, the test unsets
' every provider Detect variable but PRESERVES infrastructure ones such as
' DOCKER_HOST, which may point at a remote daemon.
'
' ── WHAT THE CREDENTIAL SUITE ASSERTS, AND OVER WHAT VALUES ─────────────────
' Every value is random per run (`tst_` + 24 random bytes, hex), so no real secret
' is involved and a leak is unambiguous; a mismatch is reported as sha256 prefix +
' length, never by printing the key. Two complementary halves:
'   * isolation (every agent, deterministic) — one `--print` subtest per agent. The
'     dry-run builds the agent's docker command through the exact same env code path
'     as a real run, so it is faithful and needs no containers.
'   * egress integrity (live) — one firewall run whose broker.env, bind-mounted into
'     the egress-proxy, must receive each key byte-for-byte. broker.env is
'     agent-independent, so one agent proves the path.
' claudecode drives the live half, not cursor: cursor declares capabilities
' egress:[open] credentials:[forward], so it has no MITM and therefore no broker at
' all. claudecode declares providers:[anthropic], which narrows the detected set to
' one — and that is precisely what ARMS the broker; with every key detected the
' broker refuses to pin and there is no broker.env to inspect.
' cursor's exception is asserted separately, and as declared capabilities rather
' than a target-name special case: the default forwards the REAL key as a bare
' `-e CURSOR_API_KEY`, with no sentinel and no --internal network, and an explicitly
' requested unsupported axis is REFUSED naming what IS allowed ("does not support
' --egress-mode allowlist (allowed: open)") — not silently rewritten, and no longer
' a warn-and-continue that hands over a dead sentinel.
' The project-.env variant proves the same boundary from the other end: a key that
' exists ONLY in the workspace .env reaches the egress sidecar byte-for-byte while
' the agent sees the sentinel and an EMPTY /app/.env. It runs against a lightweight
' probe image (ubuntu:24.04) so the credential path does not depend on any agent
' CLI's release or install state.
'
' ── WHY SOME LAYER-4 CASES HAVE NO CONTAINER AT ALL ─────────────────────────
' `proveo init --print` is the one mode that must be safe on any host: it resolves
' the plan and reports this machine's verdict without downloading, installing,
' signing in, or writing anything. Running it against the REAL host is the only way
' to catch a plan that resolves fine in a table test but names a path or a distro
' that does not exist here — and the assertion is symmetric, checking both that the
' output names the pinned release / the asset / the host triple AND that the prefix
' was never created and nothing was left in the working directory. The provenance
' case is Layer 4 for the same reason: the digest is fetched from the LIVE release
' at install time, so a bumped Release with a moved subject name would only fail on
' a host nobody is watching. Details of what init plans are in
' _spec/cmd/proveo/init-sbx-bootstrap.puml.
'
' ── THE INVOCATIONS, AS THE SUITES THEMSELVES RECORD THEM ───────────────────
'   go test -tags=e2e ./e2e/ -run CredentialForwardingIntegrity -v
'   go test -tags=e2e ./e2e/ -run ProjectDotEnvAtEgressLayer -v -timeout 10m
'   [PROVEO_TEST_LOCAL_MODEL=gemma4] \
'     go test -tags=e2e ./e2e/ -run PromptfulE2E -v -timeout 360s
'   go test -tags=e2e ./e2e/ -run InitPrint -v
'   go test -tags=e2e ./e2e/ -run InitProvenance -v
'   go test -tags=integration ./internal/egress/ (+ PROVEO_EGRESS_INTEGRATION=1)

component "**1. Unit**\ninternal/*/*_test.go — no build tags\nGo only · deterministic\n//always//" as L1 <<app>>
component "**2. Contract**\nGo builders + goldens\nDockerRunArgs · BuildPlan · Detect · writeBrokerEnv\n//always, no Docker//" as L2 <<app>>
component "**3. Infra integration**\ninternal/egress ""//go:build integration//""\nreal containers, no model\n//-tags=integration + PROVEO_EGRESS_INTEGRATION=1//" as L3 <<async>>
component "**4. Agent E2E (promptful)**\ne2e ""//go:build e2e//"" via internal/tmux\nnon-deterministic — asserts side effects\n//-tags=e2e · skips per missing prerequisite//" as L4 <<async>>

database "cov/unit\ngo tool covdata merge → percent" as COV <<db>>

L1 ARROW_MAIN L2
L2 ARROW_MAIN L3
L3 ARROW_MAIN L4
L1 ARROW_QUEUE COV
L2 ARROW_QUEUE COV

note right of L1
  Host orchestration under test is **Go**
  (""cmd/proveo"", ""internal/{runner,workspace,egress,
  sbx,gitidentity,manifest}""). Fat bash runners and the
  ""defs/lib/egress.sh"" lifecycle are retired.
end note

note right of L2
  **Go-first contracts:** execute the builders and diff
  the argv/plan, rather than grepping source for literal
  strings. A grep contract survives the deletion of the
  thing it claimed to test — it passes while testing
  nothing. Bash remains only for image smoke suites.
end note

note right of L4
  **Know which rendering an assertion reads.**
  Two backends render one launch, so a docker-only
  expectation applied to both stays red on the
  sandbox; and a restated image tag stops matching
  the container the day the tag policy moves.
end note

note right of L3
  Topology is owned (""BuildPlan"" + ""ExecRunner""), so do
  **not** add testcontainers-go for Squid/proxy. Apply
  its //practices// instead: wait on the CA file / HTTP
  ready (never sleep-only), ""t.Cleanup"" teardown, env
  gate + ""docker"" LookPath skip.
end note

note right of L4
  Two suites, different credentials:
  * ""e2e_test.go"" — local model (Ollama), opencode,
    broker mode; keyless and CI-honest
  * ""hello_world_test.go"" — cloud model, all three
    targets, firewall + credential broker
  Both assert **side effects on the host**, never the
  model's prose. See 42-hello-world-e2e.puml.
end note

note bottom of COV
  Default ""go test ./..."" never compiles Layer 3/4 — the
  PR path stays fast and keyless. No hard coverage gate
  until a baseline exists; CI publishes the percent as an
  artifact.

  **Deferred (Stage 0b):** ""proveo/egress-proxy:cover""
  from ""go build -cover"" + a bind-mounted ""GOCOVERDIR"",
  so Layer 3 reports statement coverage from inside the
  container. Topology coverage does not require it.
end note

' --- IGNORE: layout hints (do not edit) ---
L1 -down[hidden]- L2
L3 -down[hidden]- L4
' --- END IGNORE ---
note as ECHOAGENT
  **The Layer-4 promptful E2E runs against a fixture agent, not a vendor
  CLI.** ""defs/tests/echo-agent"" is a minimal image that reads a prompt and
  calls the local model through the OpenAI-compatible endpoint
  ""--local-model"" already provides — so the suite exercises proveo's own
  wiring (mounts, egress, model routing) without a paid API key or a
  vendor's release cadence in the loop.
end note


note as VACUOUS1
  **A test that cannot fail is worse than a
  missing one.** claudecode's helpers never
  defined ""run_timeout"", so the pipeline that
  called it produced ""0"" from ""grep -c"" and the
  assertion PASSED without starting a container.
end note

note as VACUOUS2
  **Put a positive assertion first.** Two
  ""assert_failure"" checks aimed at a retired
  path: ""grep"" found no directory, failed, and
  both reported PASS having verified nothing.
end note

note as VACUOUS3
  **A suite must not build what it tests.**
  claudecode's phase 1 ran a bare ""docker
  build"", so the Dockerfile's PUBLISHED default
  base applied and overwrote the local lineage —
  surfacing as ""bun: command not found"".
end note

note as VACUOUS4
  **One missing function ended nine phases.**
  A ""sed"" for a helper that had already moved
  left an empty ""eval"", so the call exited 127
  and ""set -e"" took every later phase with it.
end note

note as VACUOUS5
  **Zero of N reads as all of N.** An ""sbx"" plan
  carries no key in argv at all, so a blind sentinel
  loop checked nothing and reported success. The
  docker branch now fails when it declared no key.
end note

note as PREREQ
  **Skip only for a missing prerequisite.** The
  ""e2e"" tag is the opt-in, so a green run with every
  test skipped is an honest statement about the
  host — and ""go test -v"" prints each reason.
end note

note as DIAGNOSE
  **Snapshot while the session is alive.** Capturing
  at the moment of failure returns 'no server
  running' and hides the exit output. Only 4 empty
  captures in a row mean dead, and ""docker ps -a""
  separates 'started and died' from 'never started'.
end note

note as TAGPICK
  **Prefer the local tag, then the published one.**
  Naming one skipped whole suites on a machine that
  had just built the thing under test — or ran the
  PUBLISHED image while the report read as local.
end note

note as ROSTER
  **Read the roster from the image.**
  A list restated in a test drifts the moment
  the roster moves: cursor and opencode logged
  10 failures against a correctly built image,
  which reads as a broken image, not a stale test.
end note

note as DEFTESTS
  **Each def ships its own container test suite**, and they share a shape:
  a helper that runs a command inside the built image and captures output
  for assertions, plus a portable timeout — GNU ships ""timeout"", macOS
  ships it as ""gtimeout"" via coreutils, and where neither exists the suite
  degrades rather than failing on the tooling.

  Two habits worth keeping when editing them. The entrypoint is exercised
  by mounting a fixture project and **short-circuiting the final exec**
  (an unsupported flag that exits immediately), so a TUI launch does not
  hang a test. And several assertions have to tolerate the harness CLI
  normalising its own config on launch — cursor rewrites
  ""~/\.cursor/cli-config\.json"" every start and backs up an invalid one as
  ""\.bad"", so a test asserting on a config file must account for the CLI
  having touched it.
end note


@enduml
